is, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n---\n\n#### Step 1 – Choose two non‑sunny lines  \n\nTake  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:\\; x=1 \\qquad\\text{(vertical, parallel to the }y\\text{-axis)},\n\\]\n\\[\nL_{2}:\\; y=1 \\qquad\\text{(horizontal, parallel to the }x\\text{-axis)}.\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{2}\\) are **non‑sunny** by definition.\n\nThe points covered by these two lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{Line} & \\text{Points of }\\mathcal{P}\\text{ on the line} \\\\ \\hline\nL_{1}: x=1 & (1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3)\\\\\nL_{2}: y=1 & (1,1),\\;(2,1),\\;(3,1)\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus the only point of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) not yet covered is \\((2,2)\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Step 2 – Choose a sunny line that contains \\((2,2)\\)\n\nWe need a line through \\((2,2)\\) whose slope is **neither** \\(0\\) (horizontal), **nor** undefined (vertical), **nor** \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\nConsider the line through \\((2,2)\\) and \\((1,1)\\). Its slope is  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{2-1}{2-1}=1,\n\\]\n\nso its equation is  \n\n\\[\nL_{3}:\\; y = x .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m=1\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\), the line \\(L_{3}\\) is **sunny**.\n\n\\(L_{3}\\) passes through the points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(2,2),\n\\]\n\nboth belonging to \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Step 3 – Verify that every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) is covered\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n\\text{Point} & \\text{Line containing it} & \\text{Reason} \\\\ \\hline\n(1,1) & L_{1},L_{2},L_{3} & x=1,\\; y=1,\\; y=x \\\\\n(1,2) & L_{1} & x=1 \\\\\n(1,3) & L_{1} & x=1 \\\\\n(2,1) & L_{2} & y=1 \\\\\n(3,1) & L_{2} & y=1 \\\\\n(2,2) & L_{3} & y=x\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAll six required points lie on at least one of the three lines.\n\n---\n\n#### Step 4 – Count sunny lines\n\nAmong \\(\\{L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\}\\) only \\(L_{3}\\) is sunny; \\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{2}\\) are non‑sunny.  \nHence the configuration contains exactly \\(k=1\\) sunny line.\n\nThus for \\(n=3\\) a configuration with exactly one sunny line exists. ∎\n\n\n\