ne must be one of the six lines in (1) and none of them allow the two sunny lines to finish the covering, a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does not exist for \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, a configuration with }k=2\\text{ sunny lines is impossible.}}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nP=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points that must lie on at least one of the three lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line (i.e. a line whose slope is not \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)) contains at most two points of \\(P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(L\\) contains three distinct points of \\(P\\).  \nThe slope of the line through any two points \\((x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2)\\in P\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}.\n\\]\n\nAll possible differences \\((\\Delta x,\\Delta y)\\) with \\(\\Delta x,\\Delta y\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\) and \\(\\Delta x+\\Delta y\\le 3\\) are listed below; the corresponding slopes are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n(\\Delta x,\\Delta y) & m\\\\ \\hline\n(0,1) & \\infty\\\\\n(0,2) & \\infty\\\\\n(1,0) & 0\\\\\n(2,0) & 0\\\\\n(1,1) & 1\\\\\n(2,2) & 1\\\\\n(1,2) & 2\\\\\n(2,1) & \\tfrac12\\\\\n(1,-1) & -1\\\\\n(2,-2) & -1 .\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus any line determined by two points of \\(P\\) has slope belonging to the set  \n\\(\\{0,\\infty,-1,1,2,\\tfrac12\\}\\).  \nThe only slopes that are **allowed** for a sunny line are \\(1,2,\\tfrac12\\).  \n\nNow examine all triples of points of \\(P\\).  \nA direct check shows that the only collinear triples are\n\n* \\((1,1),(2,2),(3,3)\\) – slope \\(1\\) (sunny) but \\((3,3)\\notin P\\);\n* \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\) – slope \\(0\\) (forbidden);\n* \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) – slope \\(\\infty\\) (forbidden);\n* \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) – slope \\(-1\\) (forbidden).\n\nHence any three points of \\(P\\) are either non‑collinear or lie on a line whose slope is