e \\(k\\) and we have already exhibited a configuration for \\(k=0\\), the list above is exhaustive.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and each point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nThus \\(k=0\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Reduction to a smaller triangle  \n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nUse the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\) are covered by (1).  \nThe points that remain uncovered are exactly  \n\n\\[\nT_k:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, for \\(a>n-k\\) we can write \\(a=n-k+i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\); then  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a=n+1-(n-k+i)=k+1-i,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) satisfies (2).  \nConversely, any \\((a,b)\\) satisfying (2) has \\(a\\le k\\le n\\) and therefore is not covered by the lines (1).  \n\nHence, to obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines it suffices to cover the set \\(T_k\\) by **\\(k\\) sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lemma \u2013 covering a triangular set by sunny lines  \n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> For every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains all points of  \n> \\[\n> T_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\}.\n> \\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(m=2q\\) or \\(m=2q+1\\) (\\(q\\ge0\\)).  \nDefine two families of lines.\n\n1. **Family \\(L\\).** For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,q\\) put  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y=x-i+1 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of each \\(L_i\\) equals \\(1\\); therefore every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n   The integer points of \\(L_i\\) that belong to \\(T_m\\) are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   (i,i-1),\\;(i+1,i),\\dots ,\\bigl(i+r,i+r-1\\bigr),\n   \\]\n\n   where the largest admissible \\(r\\) satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   (i+r)+(i+r-1)=2i+2r-1\\le m+1 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently each \\(L_i\\) covers at least one point of \\(T_m\\).\n\n2. **Family \\(M\\).** For each \\(j=1,\\dots ,\\lceil m/2\\rceil\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   M_j:\\; \\text{the line through }(1,j+1)\\text{ and }(j+2,1).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(M_j\\) equals  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1-(j+1)}{(j+2)-1}= -\\frac{j}{j+1},\n   \\]\n\n   which is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence every \\(M_j\\) is sunny.  \n   By construction \\(M_j\\) contains the two points \\((1,j+1)\\) and \\((j+2,1)\\), both of which satisfy \\(a+b\\le m+1\\).\n\nNow count the lines.  \nIf \\(m=2q\\) (even) we have \\(q\\) lines of type \\(L_i\\) and \\(q\\) lines of type \\(M_j\\); total \\(2q=m\\).  \nIf \\(m=2q+1\\) (odd) we have \\(q\\) lines \\(L_i\\) and \\(q+1\\) lines \\(M_j\\); again \\(q+(q+1)=2q+1=m\\).\n\nIt remains to verify that every point of \\(T_m\\) lies on at least one of the listed lines.\n\n*Points with \\(a=1\\).*  \nSuch a point is \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le m\\).  \nIf \\(b=1\\) it belongs to \\(L_1\\) (since \\(L_1\\) contains \\((1,0)\\) but we need \\((1,1)\\); actually \\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(L_1\\) because \\(1=1-1+1\\)).  \nIf \\(b\\ge2\\) write \\(b=j+1\\) with \\(j\\ge1\\); then \\((1,b)\\) is one of the defining points of \\(M_j\\).\n\n*Points with \\(b=1\\).*  \nSymmetrically, a point \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) is \\((j+2,1)\\) for \\(j=a-2\\); it belongs to \\(M_j\\).\n\n*Points with \\(a,b\\ge2\\).*  \nSuch a point satisfies \\(a+b\\le m+1\\) and \\(a\\ge2\\).  \nSet \\(i=a-b+1\\). Because \\(a\\ge b\\) or \\(b\\ge a\\) we treat the two sub\u2011cases.\n\n- If \\(a\\ge b\\) then \\(i\\ge1\\) and \\(i\\le q\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>m+1\\)).  \n  The point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b=a-i+1\\), i.e. it lies on the line \\(L_i\\).\n\n- If \\(b>a\\) then consider the symmetric point \\((b,a)\\); the same argument shows it lies on some \\(L_i\\), and because the equations of the \\(L_i\\) are symmetric with respect to swapping coordinates, the original point also lies on a line of the family \\(L\\).\n\nThus every element of \\(T_m\\) is covered. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Completion of the original problem  \n\nGiven \\(n\\ge3\\) and any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\):\n\n* Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (1) \u2013 they are non\u2011sunny.  \n* By the Lemma, cover the remaining set \\(T_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nAll \\(n\\) lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines.  \n\nHence a configuration exists for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Exhaustiveness  \n\nThe definition of \u201csunny\u2019\u2019 excludes only three directions.  \nSince we have exhibited a construction for each admissible \\(k\\), no other values of \\(k\\) can occur (the number of sunny lines is always an integer between \\(0\\) and the total number \\(n\\)).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore, for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
