ever \\(0\\), never \\(\\infty\\), and\n   \\[\n   \\alpha_j=-1\\iff \\frac{j}{k+1-j}=-1\\iff j=-(k+1-j),\n   \\]\n   which has no solution in positive integers.  Hence every \\(S_j\\) is sunny.\n\n   *Step 4.4 – covering property.*  \n   For a fixed column \\(i\\;(v+1\\le i\\le n)\\) the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_j\\) intersect that column at the points\n   \\[\n   P_{i,j}=\\bigl(i,\\; \\alpha_j i+\\beta_j\\bigr)\n          =\\bigl(i,\\; \\tfrac{j i}{k+1-j}+1-\\tfrac{j}{k+1-j}\\bigr)\n          =\\bigl(i,\\; \\tfrac{j(i-1)}{k+1-j}+1\\bigr).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Because the denominator \\(k+1-j\\) runs through the set \\(\\{k,\\;k-1,\\dots ,1\\}\\) when \\(j\\) runs from \\(1\\) to \\(k\\), the numbers\n   \\[\n   y_{i,j}= \\frac{j(i-1)}{k+1-j}+1\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,k)\n   \\]\n   are **pairwise distinct**.  Moreover,\n   \\[\n   1\\le y_{i,1}<y_{i,2}<\\dots <y_{i,k}\\le n+1-i .\n   \\]\n   The left inequality follows from \\(j\\ge1\\) and \\(i\\ge v+1\\ge2\\); the right inequality is a short computation:\n   \\[\n   y_{i,k}= \\frac{k(i-1)}{1}+1 = k(i-1)+1\\le k(n-v-1)+1\n          = k(k-1)+1 = k^2-k+1\\le n+1-i,\n   \\]\n   where we used \\(i\\le n\\) and the identity \\(k=n-v\\).  Consequently each column \\(i\\) contains **exactly** the \\(k\\) points \\(\\{P_{i,1},\\dots ,P_{i,k}\\}\\), all of which are integer because the fraction \\(\\frac{j(i-1)}{k+1-j}\\) simplifies to an integer (the denominator divides the numerator: \\(k+1-j\\mid j(i-1)\\) because \\(\\gcd(j,k+1-j)=1\\) and \\(k+1-j\\mid (i-1)k\\) with \\(i-1\\le k\\)).  Hence every point of \\(C_i\\) appears among the \\(P_{i,j}\\); in other words the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover **all** points of the remaining region.\n\n   *Step 4.5 – distinctness of the lines.*  \n   The slopes \\(\\alpha_j\\) are distinct because the map \\(j\\mapsto \\frac{j}{k+1-j}\\) is strictly increasing on \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  Therefore the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are pairwise different, and none coincides with any of the ver