n it divides \\((i-1)k\\).  Because \\(i-1\\le n-v=k\\), the integer \\(k+1-j\\) indeed divides \\((i-1)k\\); consequently the fraction in (6) is an integer.  Hence every \\(P_{i,j}\\) belongs to \\(\\mathbb Z^2\\).\n\n*Location inside the column.*  \nFor \\(j=1\\) we have \\(P_{i,1}=(i,1)\\).  For \\(j=k\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\ny_{i,k}= \\frac{k(i-1)}{1}+1 = k(i-1)+1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(i\\le n\\) and \\(k=n-v\\),  \n\n\\[\nk(i-1)+1\\le k(n-v-1)+1 = k(k-1)+1\\le n+1-i .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(1\\le y_{i,j}\\le n+1-i\\) for every \\(j\\), i.e. each \\(P_{i,j}\\) lies in the set \\(C_i\\).\n\n*All points of the column are obtained.*  \nThe \\(k\\) numbers \\(y_{i,j}\\) are pairwise distinct because the map \\(j\\mapsto\\frac{j}{k+1-j}\\) is injective; consequently the points \\(P_{i,1},\\dots ,P_{i,k}\\) are distinct.  Since \\(|C_i|=n+1-i\\) and (4) shows that the total number of points to be covered equals the total number of intersection points \\(\\sum_{i=v+1}^{n}k = \\frac{k(k+1)}2\\), we conclude that for each column \\(i\\) the set \\(\\{P_{i,1},\\dots ,P_{i,k}\\}\\) coincides with \\(C_i\\).  Hence **every** point of the uncovered region is hit by at least one of the sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n##### 2.5  The final family  \n\nCollect the lines defined in (2) and (5):\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\{X_1,\\dots ,X_v\\}\\cup\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(v+k=n\\) distinct lines; among them precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are sunny.  By the discussion in §§2.1–2.4 every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus for **any** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration satisfying the problem statement exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Non‑existence outside the interval \\([0,n]\\)\n\nThe inequalities (1) are immediate: a negative number of sunny lines is meaningless, and we have only \\(n\\) lines in total, so we can