 intersection of \\(L_j\\) with that\n   column:\n\n   \\[\n   P_{x,j}= \\bigl(x,\\; \\tfrac{j(x-1)}{k+1-j}+1\\bigr).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(\\gcd\\bigl(j,k+1-j\\bigr)=1\\), the denominator \\(k+1-j\\) divides\n   \\(j(x-1)\\) exactly when it divides \\((x-1)k\\).  Since \\(x-1\\le k-1\\) and\n   \\(k+1-j\\le k\\), the integer \\(k+1-j\\) indeed divides \\((x-1)k\\); consequently the\n   fraction in (2) is an integer.  Hence every point \\(P_{x,j}\\) belongs to the integer\n   lattice.\n\n   *Step 3.5 – covering the whole triangle.*  \n   Fix a column \\(x\\).  The set \\(\\{P_{x,1},\\dots ,P_{x,k}\\}\\) consists of \\(k\\) distinct\n   points (the slopes are distinct, so the \\(y\\)-coordinates are distinct).  Their\n   \\(y\\)-coordinates are\n\n   \\[\n   y_{x,j}=1+\\frac{j(x-1)}{k+1-j}\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,k).\n   \\]\n\n   A short calculation shows  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le y_{x,1}<y_{x,2}<\\dots <y_{x,k}=k(x-1)+1\\le k+1-x .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The right‑most inequality follows from \\(x\\le k\\) and the identity\n   \\(k(x-1)+1\\le k+1-x\\) which is equivalent to \\((k-x)(x-1)\\ge 0\\) and is true for\n   \\(1\\le x\\le k\\).  Therefore every integer \\(y\\) with \\(1\\le y\\le k+1-x\\) appears as\n   \\(y_{x,j}\\) for a suitable \\(j\\).  In other words,\n\n   \\[\n   \\{P_{x,1},\\dots ,P_{x,k}\\}= \\{(x,y)\\mid 1\\le y\\le k+1-x\\}=C_x .\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) cover **all** points of the\n   triangle \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\).\n\n   *Step 3.6 – the final family of \\(n\\) lines.*  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L:=\\{X_1,\\dots ,X_v\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly \\(v+k=n\\) distinct lines; precisely the\n   \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are sunny.  By the discussion in Steps 3.1–3.5,\n   every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n   Hence for **any** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n4. **Impossibility outside the interval.**  \n   A negative number of sunny lines is meaningless, and we cannot have more than\n   \\(n\\) sunny lines because we have only \\(n\\) lines in total.  Thus no\n   \\(k\\notin\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is possible.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the explicit constructions for all admissible \\(k\\) with the trivial\n   bounds yields the complete answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\nWe are given an integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and asked for all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for\nwhich there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying  \n\n* every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Trivial bounds\n\nBecause we have precisely \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines cannot\nexceed \\(n\\); it also cannot be negative.  Hence\n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible \\(k\\) must belong to the set \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nv:=n-k\\qquad(0\\le v\\le n).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Non‑sunny vertical lines\n\nTake the \\(v\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nX_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,v). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(X_i\\) is non‑sunny (its slope is \\(\\infty\\)).  \nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose first coordinate is \\(\\le v\\) lie on one of\nthe lines \\(X_i\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The remaining triangular region\n\nAfter removing the points already covered, the uncovered set is\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q_k\n   =\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid 1\\le x\\le k,\\;1\\le y\\le k+1-x\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) is a right‑angled triangle of side‑length \\(k\\); it\ncontains  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal Q_k|=\\sum_{x=1}^{k}(k+1-x)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Sunny lines covering the triangle\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\;y=\\frac{j}{k+1-j}\\,x+1-\\frac{j}{k+1-j}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*The slope* \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{j}{k+1-j}\\) is a positive rational number; it is\nneither \\(0\\) nor \\(\\infty\\) and it is never \\(-1\\) because the equation\n\\(\\frac{j}{k+1-j}=-1\\) would give \\(j=-(k+1-j)\\), impossible for positive\n\\(j\\).  Hence every \\(L_j\\) is sunny.\n\nIf \\(j_1\\neq j_2\\) then \\(\\frac{j_1}{k+1-j_1}\\neq\\frac{j_2}{k+1-j_2}\\); therefore the\nslopes are distinct and the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are pairwise different.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Integer points on the lines\n\nFix a column \\(x\\) with \\(1\\le x\\le k\\).  Substituting \\(x\\) into (5) gives the\nintersection point\n\n\\[\nP_{x,j}= \\Bigl(x,\\; \\frac{j(x-1)}{k+1-j}+1\\Bigr),\\qquad j=1,\\dots ,k.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\gcd\\bigl(j,k+1-j\\bigr)=1\\), the denominator \\(k+1-j\\) divides the\nproduct \\(j(x-1)\\) exactly when it divides \\((x-1)k\\).  Since \\(x-1\\le k-1\\) and\n\\(k+1-j\\le k\\), the integer \\(k+1-j\\) indeed divides \\((x-1)k\\); consequently the\nfraction in (6) is an integer.  Thus every \\(P_{x,j}\\) belongs to the integer\nlattice.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5  The set of points in a column\n\nFor the fixed column \\(x\\) define  \n\n\\[\ny_{x,j}=1+\\frac{j(x-1)}{k+1-j}\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe numbers \\(y_{x,1},\\dots ,y_{x,k}\\) are pairwise distinct because the\nslopes are distinct.  Moreover,\n\n\\[\n1\\le y_{x,1}<y_{x,2}<\\dots <y_{x,k}=k(x-1)+1. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x\\le k\\),\n\n\\[\nk(x-1)+1\\le k+1-x,\n\\]\n\nwhich is equivalent to \\((k-x)(x-1)\\ge0\\) and holds for all \\(1\\le x\\le k\\).\nHence (8) implies\n\n\\[\n\\{y_{x,1},\\dots ,y_{x,k}\\}= \\{1,2,\\dots ,k+1-x\\}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nCombining (6) and (9) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\{P_{x,1},\\dots ,P_{x,k}\\}= \\{(x,y)\\mid 1\\le y\\le k+1-x\\}=C_x,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(C_x\\) denotes the set of points of \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) lying in column \\(x\\).\nThus **every** point of the triangle \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) lies on one of the lines\n\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.6  The final family of \\(n\\) lines\n\nCollect the lines defined in (2) and (5):\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\{X_1,\\dots ,X_v\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(v+k=n\\) distinct lines; precisely the\n\\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are sunny.  By Sections 2.1 and 2.5 every point of\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nHence for the chosen \\(k\\) a configuration satisfying the two required\nconditions exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Non‑existence for \\(k\\notin\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)\n\nIf \\(k<0\\) the statement “exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines’’ is meaningless.  \nIf \\(k>n\\) we would need more sunny lines than the total number \\(n\\) of lines,\nwhich is impossible.  Therefore no such \\(k\\) can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nBoth the lower bound (\\(k