nts of \\(P_i\\) determine\n      a unique line; denote this line by \\(L_i\\).  Since the points of \\(P_i\\) never\n      share the same abscissa, the slope of \\(L_i\\) is finite; because we never\n      placed two points with a difference of coordinates \\((\\Delta x,\\Delta y)\\)\n      satisfying \\(\\Delta y=-\\Delta x\\), the slope is never \\(-1\\); finally the\n      points never have the same ordinate, so the slope is never \\(0\\).  Hence each\n      \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\n   3. **Coverage.**  \n      By construction every point of \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) belongs to some set \\(P_i\\) and\n      therefore lies on the corresponding line \\(L_i\\).  The lines are distinct\n      because their slopes are distinct (if two lines coincided they would contain\n      the same ordered pair of points, contradicting the bijective assignment).\n\n   This completes the proof of Lemma 1. ∎\n\n   *Step 3.4 – assembling the whole family.*  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L:=\\{X_1,\\dots ,X_v\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(v+k=n\\) distinct lines.\n   Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are sunny, while the \\(v\\) vertical\n   lines are non‑sunny.  By Step 3.1 the vertical lines cover all points of\n   \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with first coordinate \\(\\le v\\); by Lemma 1 the sunny lines\n   cover every point of the remaining set \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\).  Consequently every\n   point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n   Hence for **any** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration satisfying\n   the two required conditions exists.\n\n4. **Impossibility outside the interval.**  \n   A negative number of sunny lines is meaningless, and we cannot have more than\n   \\(n\\) sunny lines because there are only \\(n\\) lines in total.  Therefore no\n   integer \\(k\\notin\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) can occur.\n\n5. **Conclusion**  \n   Combining the explicit construction for every admissible \\(k\\) with the trivial\n   bounds (1) yields the complete answer  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]   ∎"
