n   points of the column.  \n\n   1. **Assignment of points.**  \n      For column \\(x\\) write the admissible set of ordinates as  \n\n      \\[\n      C_x:=\\{1,2,\\dots ,k+1-x\\}.\n      \\]\n\n      Choose a bijection  \n\n      \\[\n      \\phi_x:C_x\\longrightarrow\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\n      \\]\n\n      such that for every line index \\(i\\) the point \\((x,\\phi_x^{-1}(i))\\) is\n      defined (if \\(i\\notin\\phi_x(C_x)\\) we simply do not assign a point of\n      column \\(x\\) to line \\(i\\)).  \n      The bijections are chosen inductively so that no line ever receives two\n      points with the same abscissa (which would give a vertical line) and never\n      receives two points \\((x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2)\\) with \\(y_2-y_1=-(x_2-x_1)\\)\n      (which would give slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\n      This can be done because in column \\(x\\) we have at most \\(k+1-x\\le k\\)\n      points to distribute among the \\(k\\) lines, and the forbidden\n      configurations involve at most one choice for each line.  A simple greedy\n      algorithm works: process the columns in the order \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,k\\); when\n      column \\(x\\) is considered, each line \\(i\\) already contains at most one\n      point, so there are at most one forbidden ordinate for line \\(i\\) (the one\n      that would create slope \\(-1\\) with the existing point of that line).  Since\n      \\(|C_x|\\le k\\), we can always pick a distinct admissible ordinate for each\n      line that still needs a point in this column.  Thus the assignment succeeds\n      and yields a family of points  \n\n      \\[\n      P_i:=\\{(x,y)\\mid x\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\},\\;y=\\phi_x^{-1}(i)\\}\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k).\n      \\tag{5}\n      \\]\n\n      By construction each column \\(x\\) contributes exactly the set \\(C_x\\) to the\n      union \\(\\bigcup_i P_i\\).\n\n   2. **Definition of the lines.**  \n      For each \\(i\\) the set \\(P_i\\) contains at least two points (because\n      \\(|C_x|\\ge1\\) for every 